Subject: [PATCH] compose: proxy credentials alone do not replace the device

An --apostate-profile payload that carries only proxy_credentials, with
an empty or missing device_profile, does not describe a device. The
browser composes a profile as usual and keeps the credentials with it.
Otherwise an authenticated proxy launch would present the host on every
value a page reads and ignore --fingerprint. Credentials are read with
or without the device_profile wrapper. A payload with any device content
wins over composition, and --fingerprint-explain lists the switches it
makes inert.

--- a/base/apostate/profile.h
+++ b/base/apostate/profile.h
@@ -60,6 +60,44 @@
 
   // Overrides the profile for tests. Passing nullptr clears it.
   static void SetForTesting(const Profile* profile);
+
+  // True when an `--apostate-profile` payload actually claims a device.
+  //
+  // The payload is either a bare profile or the two-key envelope
+  // `{device_profile, proxy_credentials}`. Proxy credentials have no switch of
+  // their own on purpose -- argv is world-readable in `ps` output, and
+  // docs/PROFILE_SPEC.md keeps them off the command line for that reason -- so
+  // the envelope is their only channel, and a launch that authenticates to a
+  // proxy while authoring no profile sends one whose `device_profile` is
+  // empty. That payload describes no device, and a payload that describes no
+  // device must not stand in for the composed one.
+  //
+  // Precisely: for the envelope form, `device_profile` is non-empty; for the
+  // bare form, some key other than `proxy_credentials` is present. A payload
+  // with one authored surface in it does claim a device and still wins
+  // outright, absent fields staying absent on every other axis. That is what
+  // makes a single-surface payload usable for testing, and it is why this is a
+  // predicate rather than a merge of the payload over the composition.
+  //
+  // A payload that is not base64, or not a JSON object, counts as a device
+  // claim. Its content is undecidable here and Get() refuses it; answering
+  // "no device" instead would replace a malformed operator-supplied profile
+  // with a composed one and launch happily, which is the outcome that refusal
+  // exists to prevent.
+  static bool PayloadDescribesDevice(const std::string& encoded);
+
+  // `composed` re-enveloped so that it carries the `proxy_credentials` block
+  // `supplied` carried.
+  //
+  // Returns nullopt when `supplied` carried no credentials, when either
+  // payload cannot be decoded, or when the result cannot be serialised. In
+  // each of those cases the caller installs `composed` as it stands: a
+  // composed device without credentials is a launch that fails to authenticate
+  // loudly at the proxy, and a host-inherited fingerprint is one that fails
+  // silently at every site.
+  static std::optional<std::string> PayloadWithSuppliedCredentials(
+      const std::string& supplied,
+      const std::string& composed);
 
   Profile();
   ~Profile();
